id: rule-skip-different
title: Different assertions
topic: Skip
example-of: SKIP
derived: false

task:
  Use the rule SKIP to decide whether this triple can be derived.

pre: x = 0
post: x = 1

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  skip       // s
  {Q}

verdict: invalid

step: R1, the precondition given by the rule SKIP
rule: SKIP
focus: s
text:
  The rule SKIP gives the triple whose precondition is the postcondition:
  `skip` does not change the state.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = Q           // wp(skip, Q) = Q
  = x = 1       // Q is x = 1
set: R1 = x = 1

step: P does not imply R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  The precondition $P$ is not the formula $R1$. The consequence rule would
  need $P$ to imply $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: x = 0 ==> x = 1
status: fails
counterexample: x = 0
why:
  In the state $x = 0$, `skip` does not change $x$, so $x = 1$ is false at
  the end. The triple is not valid.
